{{also|conjuré}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|conjurer||lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|coniūrō||I swear together; conspire}}, from {{term|con-||with, together|lang=la}} + {{term|iūro||I swear or take an oath|lang=la|}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkʌnd͡ʒər/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʌndʒə(r)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|conjur}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[perform]] [[magic]] [[trick]]s.
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[summon]] up using [[supernatural]] [[power]], as a [[devil]]
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[practice]] [[black magic]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[evoke]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[imagine]] or [[picture]] in the [[mind]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To make an [[urgent]] [[request]] to; to [[appeal]] to or [[beseech]].
#* Addison
#*: I '''conjure''' you, let him know, / Whate'er was done against him, Cato did it.
#* '''1851''', Herman Melville, ''Moby-Dick'':
#*: Stammering out something, I knew not what, I rolled away from him against the wall, and then '''conjured''' him, whoever or whatever he might be, to keep quiet, and let me get up and light the lamp again.
# {{context|intransitive|obsolete|lang=en}} To [[conspire]] or [[plot]]. <!--a Latinism-->
#* Milton
#*: Drew after him the third part of Heaven's sons / '''Conjured''' against the Highest.

